+ASTRA KARDASHEV GENESIS — Establish or Falsify Passive Mechanical De-Trapping
+Continue from your mechanically constrained ionic-trap model, coupled useful/harmful transition analysis, complete occupancy–throughput calculation and prior-art findings.
+Mission: Determine whether passive mechanical constraint provides a genuinely new, physically obtainable operation that improves useful ionic transport and suppresses destructive material processes in the same operating regime.
+Your previous calculation established a conditional opportunity: a shared constraint can lower the useful escape barrier while raising a harmful transition barrier when the accessible relaxation energies have the required ordering.
+It also demonstrated that improved escape can reduce total transport when entry is limiting.
+The next objective is not to further optimize hypothetical response energies.
+Find or rule out a credible real material–support system that satisfies the complete mechanism.
+Investigate experimentally established candidate materials, accessible structural data, first-principles results, local mechanical compliance and realistic support geometries.
+Determine whether the relevant occupied, empty, useful-transition and destructive-transition responses can be obtained from the same material model under the same boundary conditions.
+Where published data are insufficient, identify the exact calculation or experiment needed. Do not treat an arbitrary frozen-atom simulation as an accessible passive mechanical constraint.
+Include competing entry pathways, state occupancy, kinetic prefactors, damage processes, geometry, support-induced chemistry, charge balance, and full transport consequences.
+Compare with the strongest existing strain engineering, defect chemistry, pore modification and materials-processing alternatives.
+The important technological output is increased useful transport or lifetime at acceptable complete cost—not a favourable barrier shift in isolation.
+If a credible implementation exists, develop it deeply and establish a decisive material experiment with realistic operating thresholds.
+If no candidate survives, determine whether the mechanism is blocked by an intrinsic physical coupling, an unacquired material response or the existence of a stronger established operation.
+Do not repeatedly replace measured coefficients with synthetic favourable values.
+Preserve the scientifically useful mathematical result even if the proposed technology fails.
+Establish whether this coupled mechanical operation creates real technological leverage—or stop investing in its hypothetical implementation.
